---
// The home page: two equal doors (Web Dev Ask "The site refocused on the
// written record", §4.3, Release B; the Editor's rulings 12 and 20 of
// 12 Nov 2026, and of 13 Nov 2026, "1-4 as recommended"). Top to bottom:
//   1. the opening line, in the Editor's voice, as the page's h1;
//   2. two panels of equal weight, side by side on a wide screen and stacked
//      on a phone: Commentary (the units as text cards, and a "Read the
//      commentary" button) and Articles (each article's title, provenance and
//      description, and a "Read the articles" button);
//   3. the Latest strip: the two newest pages of either kind, from their own
//      dates (two by the Editor's word of 13 Nov 2026);
//   4. the description paragraphs (the first conformed, §8 item (a)), the
//      Editor's welcome, and the About links.
//
// It revises the page of PR #109 and the Editor's changes of 13 Nov 2026:
// the text-only unit cards are kept; the "Watch the lectures" button is gone
// (the lectures are reached by each commentary page's "Watch the lecture"
// link); and the Articles list that stood after Latest becomes the second
// panel.
//
// NOTHING HERE IS LISTED BY HAND. The volumes, units, articles and the Latest
// strip are read from the content collections, and `isVisible` gates every
// one of them, so a draft shows nothing and a newly published page appears
// without an edit here. Until an article is published the Articles panel is
// omitted and the Commentary panel takes the full width.
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content';
import { SITE, commentaryUrl, articleUrl, formatDate, isVisible } from '../lib/site';

const volumes = (await getCollection('volumes'))
  .filter(isVisible)
  .sort((a, b) => a.data.title.localeCompare(b.data.title));
const allCommentary = (await getCollection('commentary')).filter(isVisible);

// Reading order: chapter, then first verse (the unit), then section number.
const byReadingOrder = (
  a: (typeof allCommentary)[number],
  b: (typeof allCommentary)[number]
) =>
  a.data.chapter - b.data.chapter ||
  (a.data.verseStart ?? 0) - (b.data.verseStart ?? 0) ||
  a.data.sectionNumber - b.data.sectionNumber;

// --- Commentary: the units as cards -------------------------------------------
// Each published unit landing of each published volume, in reading order,
// as a text card: verses, title, and the reading link (no picture, by the
// Editor's ruling of 13 Nov 2026 and §4.3).
const bookDisplayOf = new Map(volumes.map((v) => [v.data.book, v.data.bookDisplay]));
const volumeUnits = volumes
  .map((v) => ({
    volume: v,
    units: allCommentary
      .filter((e) => e.data.sectionType === 'unit-landing' && e.data.book === v.data.book)
      .sort(byReadingOrder)
      .map((u) => ({
        id: u.id,
        title: u.data.title,
        verses: `${v.data.bookDisplay} ${u.data.passageRef}`,
      })),
  }))
  .filter((vu) => vu.units.length > 0);

// --- Articles ----------------------------------------------------------------
// Parent articles only (chapters excluded), newest first.
const articles = (await getCollection('articles'))
  .filter((a) => !a.id.includes('/') && isVisible(a))
  .sort((a, b) => b.data.date.valueOf() - a.data.date.valueOf());

// --- Latest ------------------------------------------------------------------
// The two most recently published pages of either kind, newest first, read
// from each page's own `date` (the publication date; the Editor's ruling,
// 12 Nov 2026). Not `revised`, which a conform sweep moves on many older
// pages at once. Commentary pages published the same day fall back to reading
// order, latest first.
const LATEST_COUNT = 2;
type Latest = { href: string; title: string; meta: string; date: Date };
const latestCommentary = [...allCommentary]
  .sort((a, b) => b.data.date.valueOf() - a.data.date.valueOf() || byReadingOrder(b, a))
  .map((e): Latest => ({
    href: commentaryUrl(e.id),
    title: e.data.title,
    meta: `${bookDisplayOf.get(e.data.book) ?? ''} ${e.data.passageRef}`.trim(),
    date: e.data.date,
  }));
const latestArticles = articles.map((a): Latest => ({
  href: articleUrl(a.id),
  title: a.data.title,
  meta: 'Article',
  date: a.data.date,
}));
// A stable merge: on a tie of dates the commentary page comes first.
const latest = [...latestCommentary, ...latestArticles]
  .map((x, i) => ({ x, i }))
  .sort((a, b) => b.x.date.valueOf() - a.x.date.valueOf() || a.i - b.i)
  .slice(0, LATEST_COUNT)
  .map(({ x }) => x);
---
<BaseLayout
  title={SITE.wordmark}
  description="Verse-by-verse commentary on the New Testament at the systematic-theological register, and scholarly articles in the field. Freely available under CC BY 4.0."
  tier="1"
>
  <div class="reading reading--wide home">
    <header class="home-hero">
      {/* The opening line is the page's h1. The series name is not set again
          here: the header's subtitle, directly above, already carries it. */}
      <h1 class="home-hero__headline">
        Verse-by-verse commentary on the New Testament, and scholarly articles
        in the field — free to read, to cite, and to reuse.
      </h1>
    </header>

    <div class={`home-doors${articles.length > 0 ? ' home-doors--two' : ''}`}>
      {volumeUnits.length > 0 && (
        <section class="home-door" aria-labelledby="door-commentary">
          <h2 id="door-commentary"><a href="/commentary/">Commentary</a></h2>
          {volumeUnits.map((vu) => (
            <Fragment>
              <p class="home-door__sub">
                <a href={commentaryUrl(vu.volume.data.book)}>{vu.volume.data.title}</a>
              </p>
              <ul class="unit-cards">
                {vu.units.map((u) => (
                  <li>
                    <a class="unit-card" href={commentaryUrl(u.id)}>
                      <span class="unit-card__verses">{u.verses}</span>
                      <span class="unit-card__title">{u.title}</span>
                      <span class="unit-card__action">Start reading</span>
                    </a>
                  </li>
                ))}
              </ul>
            </Fragment>
          ))}
          <p class="home-door__action">
            <a class="button button--primary" href={commentaryUrl(volumeUnits[0].volume.data.book)}>
              Read the commentary
            </a>
          </p>
        </section>
      )}

      {articles.length > 0 && (
        <section class="home-door" aria-labelledby="door-articles">
          <h2 id="door-articles"><a href="/articles/">Articles</a></h2>
          <ul class="article-cards">
            {articles.map((a) => (
              <li>
                <a class="unit-card" href={articleUrl(a.id)}>
                  {a.data.provenance && <span class="unit-card__verses">{a.data.provenance}</span>}
                  <span class="unit-card__title">{a.data.title}</span>
                  <span class="article-card__desc">{a.data.description}</span>
                </a>
              </li>
            ))}
          </ul>
          <p class="home-door__action">
            <a class="button button--primary" href="/articles/">Read the articles</a>
          </p>
        </section>
      )}
    </div>

    {latest.length > 0 && (
      <section class="home-latest" aria-labelledby="latest">
        <h2 id="latest">Latest</h2>
        <ol class="latest-list">
          {latest.map((p) => (
            <li>
              <a href={p.href}>{p.title}</a>
              <span class="latest-list__meta">
                {p.meta} · <time datetime={p.date.toISOString().slice(0, 10)}>{formatDate(p.date)}</time>
              </span>
            </li>
          ))}
        </ol>
      </section>
    )}

    <p>
      A verse-by-verse commentary on the New Testament, taken up across the
      range of its writings — each volume carried through to a complete whole.
      The published Commentary is paired, section by section, with audiovisual
      Lectures that teach the same scholarly work — the writing for readers at
      depth, the lectures for teachers and students, on the series' lecture
      site. Particular questions that warrant fuller treatment are developed as
      scholarly articles, published here and submitted for peer review in
      journals in the field. The series is freely available under a Creative
      Commons Attribution 4.0 license.
    </p>
    <p>
      SECNT is also an invitation into a new genre of biblical scholarship. The
      Commentary teaches the text’s own compositional architecture — the
      deliberate shape of the writing — as part of what it sets before the
      reader, not as work kept behind the page. And it is unusual in how it is
      made: every position is adjudicated and locked by a named, accountable
      editor, working in a method explained openly on these pages — the
      human-accountable use of assistance toward which scholarship across many
      fields is already trending. Begin with
      <a href="/architecture-principles/">Architecture Principles</a> and
      <a href="/methodology/">Methodology</a>, and read not only what the series
      concludes but how it reaches its conclusions.
    </p>

    <blockquote class="editor-welcome">
      <p>But most of all, my prayer is that God will speak to you and give you clarity as you seek to know Him better through our commentaries.</p>
      <footer>— Ronald E. Roades, BA, MA (Wheaton College)<br />Chief Editor, SECNT<br /><a href="mailto:editor@secnt.org">editor@secnt.org</a></footer>
    </blockquote>

    <h2>About the series</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><a href="/about/">About SECNT</a></li>
      <li><a href="/architecture-principles/">Architecture Principles</a></li>
      <li><a href="/methodology/">Methodology</a></li>
      <li><a href="/citing/">Citing</a></li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
